# Suppressions for reachable memory held by the glibc dynamic linker (ld.so)
# when a name lookup that needs DNS makes glibc load an NSS service module
# (libnss_dns.so.2, libnss_mdns4_minimal.so.2, ...). The chain is
# __nss_module_load -> module_load -> _dl_open, and it is reproducible with
# nothing but a getaddrinfo(3) call on a name the hosts file does not answer:
# such a program leaks the same seven reachable records this suite's own
# tests binary does.
#
# These blocks are ld.so's internal bookkeeping (link_map structures, strdup'd
# library path strings from the ld.so cache lookup, and symbol-version tables)
# and are intentionally kept live until process exit: glibc loads NSS modules
# through its own private loader (__libc_dlopen_mode) and never calls a
# matching dlclose-equivalent. They are not leaks.
#
# Two rules cover every variant, split only by which allocator ld.so reaches
# for. Both are exercised: the malloc rule covers the cache-lookup path
# strings and _dl_new_object's own link_map allocation, the calloc rule covers
# _dl_new_object's larger zeroed object block and _dl_check_map_versions's
# version tables.
#
# Each rule requires _dl_open itself in the stack, which is what keeps them
# from reaching anything this project allocates: these test binaries link the
# library statically, so no code of this project's ever runs underneath a
# dynamic-linker open.

{
   ld_so_dlopen_reachable_malloc
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   ...
   fun:_dl_open
}

{
   ld_so_dlopen_reachable_calloc
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:_dl_open
}
